WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.920
Hey everyone, today let's talk about hybrid cloud.&nbsp;
Now by the book it refers to a cloud computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.960
architecture with two or more environments&nbsp;
consisting of public and private clouds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.160
on-premises, and these days&nbsp;
even edge environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.800
But without the right strategy, hybrid cloud&nbsp;
can actually pose a number of challenges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.160
Now today we're going to go through an example to&nbsp;
lay out some of those advantages and disadvantages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.520
of a fictional distribution company&nbsp;
that started to undergo that hybrid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.520
cloud transformation process. Now we're going to&nbsp;
start with what they have kind of on-premises.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.760
Now right off the bat let's start with&nbsp;
this legacy application that they've got.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.200
Now this is an application that enables their&nbsp;
users to get shipment and tracking information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.400
and it continues to work so they don't really&nbsp;
have a good reason to modernize it, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.240
it is kind of running on some legacy, old school,&nbsp;
let's say Java EE based monolithic architecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.680
In addition, they've also got some customer&nbsp;
data that's running on-premises. Now this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.360
one of the key advantages of hybrid cloud is&nbsp;
it lets you run workloads where you need them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.120
So in this particular case to comply with GDPR&nbsp;
or maybe some other regulations they need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.040
keep that data on-premises which is actually a&nbsp;
pretty cool advantage of hybrid cloud that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.560
can continue to do that. Now, they've also&nbsp;
got some HR software running on-premises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.200
we're going to call this BPMS, or&nbsp;
business process management software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.840
and this is going to allow their customers to&nbsp;
do or rather their workforce to do things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.560
requesting time off, or maybe you know doing&nbsp;
some purchase work orders that kind of thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.080
So that's what they've got on-prem. Now we&nbsp;
mentioned that they've started the hybrid cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.160
transformation, they started to take advantage&nbsp;
of cloud native, so let's see what they've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:52.640
in the public cloud. Now we've distilled the cloud&nbsp;
into kind of three major categories in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.960
so Infrastructure as a Service, Platform&nbsp;
as a Service, and Software as a Service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.080
Now, let's start with PaaS and what they've done&nbsp;
here is they've taken advantage of a Kubernetes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:07.040
or container-based environment, maybe even&nbsp;
something like managed OpenShift running in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.160
the cloud to create a new version of the same&nbsp;
application we talked about before the one one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:18.000
that works for mobile users. So mobile backend&nbsp;
as well as a database there that kind of works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.440
for mobile users to be able to get tracking and&nbsp;
shipment information. But this led to an increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.040
cost and support to maintain all those users, so&nbsp;
they started to take advantage of another service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.000
available in the cloud. So here's where SaaS&nbsp;
comes in. They took a chat bot and they built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.880
it into that application and that enables them&nbsp;
to offset some of that increased support costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.360
In addition, let's say they're also using&nbsp;
some of the SaaS capabilities for IoT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.800
for their delivery drivers, maybe in their&nbsp;
warehouses, their distribution centers, that kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.440
of thing. So IoT is helping you know track the&nbsp;
goods and maybe making sure they're temperature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.120
controlled, that kind of thing. And finally, for&nbsp;
IaaS, here's an environment that I think kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.320
benefits from lift and shift. So some of&nbsp;
that business process management software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.760
that they've got running on-prem. Maybe they've&nbsp;
migrated some of that software over to the cloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.560
and I think that's an excellent opportunity to&nbsp;
start taking advantage of IaaS is by kind of doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.880
that lift and shift, taking advantage of some of&nbsp;
that software and moving it there. Maybe they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.480
also running some CMS systems like WordPress for&nbsp;
content management and they're running that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.600
software on some VMs that are made available&nbsp;
through the IaaS capabilities in the cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.560
Now those two environments are integrated together&nbsp;
because you know they've got some shared data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.040
obviously the web interface and the mobile&nbsp;
backend were probably using a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.440
data source. Finally they've also got edge&nbsp;
environments, so these are going to be things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.640
like distribution centers. They're called edge&nbsp;
environments because they're at the edge, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.800
where data is being created and these distribution&nbsp;
centers basically enable the customer to do things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.600
like kind of run workloads right where the data is&nbsp;
being created. So maybe something like Kubernetes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.840
or again OpenShift, a distribution of Kubernetes&nbsp;
to manage the edge workloads where data is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.760
being created. And those are obviously going&nbsp;
to be integrated to the on-premises and cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.720
environments to share data about shipments so&nbsp;
their users are getting the latest and greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.880
information about you know where that shipment&nbsp;
is. So this is where the the company is today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.240
and they've run into a number of challenges.&nbsp;
So number one, they have increased complexity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.720
So as they've gone from on-premises to the cloud&nbsp;
and all these engine environments now they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.800
kind of an increased number of environments,&nbsp;
so their ops teams have to maintain that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.720
In addition, they need a way to port assets&nbsp;
from one environment to another. So, so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.240
they haven't necessarily run into any issues but&nbsp;
they're starting to build a chat bot into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.880
mobile backend and say hey maybe we should start&nbsp;
taking advantage of that on our web application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.680
as well. And they're finding they're not able to&nbsp;
easily do that, their DevOps teams are struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.480
because their assets aren't quite portable, maybe&nbsp;
they have multiple vendors they're working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.200
there isn't that consistency. And the&nbsp;
last concern I'll raise is security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.320
more environments, higher surface area of attack,&nbsp;
their security team has to kind of invest more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.160
resources to help maintain these solutions. So&nbsp;
there is a solution to all of this and it's taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.400
on a unified and open hybrid cloud approach and&nbsp;
that's going to start at the infrastructure layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.680
that we've already talked about a little bit. So,&nbsp;
that infrastructure layer is going to be things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.640
like the on-premises environment, on-premises&nbsp;
environment, it's going to be public and private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.880
cloud environments, as well as edge locations.&nbsp;
But here's where the key piece kind of starts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:47.600
kind of work out. So at the foundational layer&nbsp;
they need to start building some standardization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.560
So here's where they can start building in a&nbsp;
consistent operating system. So, something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.560
Linux, or RHEL, or Red Hat Enterprise Linux, is&nbsp;
going to help here because their developers can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.000
work against a single operating system&nbsp;
across these multiple environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.320
And I mentioned RHEL because that brings me&nbsp;
to my next point here the platform layer.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.680
And here's where they're going to be&nbsp;
running something like Kubernetes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.840
Kubernetes is great because it gives you&nbsp;
that consistent container based environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.840
that works in any kind of location on-premises,&nbsp;
edge, the cloud, but you can even take it a step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.880
further with OpenShift container platform which&nbsp;
is going to give you that enterprise supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.560
approach to containers, and again,&nbsp;
it's great because it runs anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:40.080
Finally, the key to all of this is the solutions.&nbsp;
So this consistency and standardization enables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.360
customers to focus on standardized solutions&nbsp;
across the board so this can be things like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.360
apps that they're developing, or the data that&nbsp;
they need to manage, or even security solutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.320
that they're building across the board. And this&nbsp;
is just scratching the surface there's other use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:02.000
cases here that I'm not even diving into. Things&nbsp;
like integration, managing these multiple cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.840
environments, automation, that kind of thing. But&nbsp;
this standardized layer and platform enables them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.400
to focus on solutions that work across the&nbsp;
board. Now this is going to lead to a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.280
of advantages. Now the number one thing about&nbsp;
hybrid cloud is that it needs to be portable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.680
That means your workloads need to be able to move&nbsp;
across environments. Now we mentioned they've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.560
a monolithic legacy application architecture&nbsp;
on-prem, maybe they start pulling in OpenShift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:32.720
or Kubernetes on-premises, start refactoring into&nbsp;
microservices and cloud native based applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.840
and they start having containers for that web&nbsp;
interface application. Now if they want to they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:43.040
can start porting those assets to the cloud&nbsp;
and take advantage of the elasticity there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:50.320
scale up and down on demand. Number two, it's&nbsp;
going to be faster innovation. Now with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:56.480
portability their developers can now think in a&nbsp;
new way they can build once and deploy anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:00.560
So that chatbot experience they built for the&nbsp;
mobile backend they're going to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.080
readily port that to the on-premise experience to&nbsp;
the web user interface experience, the legacy app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:10.960
if they modernize it to kind of container-based&nbsp;
architecture. So that's one of the key advantages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:17.280
their developers are going to benefit from faster&nbsp;
innovation, reduce time to value, faster go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:23.360
market by taking advantage of the industry's&nbsp;
best, you know DevOps and agile practices. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:30.480
that's a key part about a hybrid cloud is faster&nbsp;
innovation. And finally, as we mentioned before on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:37.600
the security front with that standardized layer of&nbsp;
that OpenShift or Kubernetes that container-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.240
environment across the board. You're going to&nbsp;
have consistency for things like pushing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.840
compliance policies to these multiple environments&nbsp;
and that's going to be critical here to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:53.840
you have that kind of secure and consistent&nbsp;
approach across these multiple environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.040
Now in the interest of standardization&nbsp;
and standardization across the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.800
and having that kind of consistent layer.&nbsp;
Imagine if you could handle all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.240
layers, right, infrastructure foundation and&nbsp;
solutions all from a single say public cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:16.480
environment. That means extending the public&nbsp;
cloud to not just on-premises environment but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.800
maybe even other clouds and the edge.&nbsp;
Well that's when we're starting to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:25.120
into the concept of Distributed Cloud, so&nbsp;
I urge you to check out my video on that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:32.800
Now with Hybrid Cloud it's going to be paramount&nbsp;
that you take a unified and open approach to truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.080
set yourself up for success. If you enjoyed this&nbsp;
video, have any questions be sure to drop a like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.240
or comment below. Stay tuned and follow us for&nbsp;
more videos like this in the future. Thank you.

